			<!-- Section 1: The Piecewise Math -->
			<section class="mb-10 font-sans">
				<h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-4">
					1. The Piecewise Math of Philippine Transit Fares
				</h3>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					Every franchised Public Utility Vehicle (PUV) operating in the Philippines operates under tariff rates established by the <strong>Land Transportation Franchising and Regulatory Board (LTFRB)</strong> pursuant to the <strong>Public Service Act (Commonwealth Act No. 146)</strong>.
				</p>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					Unlike ride-hailing applications that use dynamic surge pricing algorithms, all public buses and jeepneys use a strict <strong>piecewise linear distance formula</strong>:
				</p>

				<div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border border-mapa-contour font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm my-6 space-y-2">
					<div class="text-mapa-clay font-sans text-xs uppercase font-bold mb-1">Standard Statutory Distance Formula:</div>
					<div class="text-mapa-green font-bold">If Distance ≤ Base Distance (4 or 5 km):</div>
					<div class="pl-4">Legal Fare = Base Fare</div>
					<div class="text-mapa-green font-bold mt-2">If Distance > Base Distance:</div>
					<div class="pl-4">Legal Fare = Base Fare + [(Distance - Base Distance) × Succeeding Per-KM Rate]</div>
				</div>

				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed">
					For example, if you board a Traditional Jeepney for a <strong>10-kilometer</strong> commute, the first 4 kilometers cost <strong>₱13.00</strong>. The remaining 6 kilometers are charged at <strong>₱1.80 per kilometer</strong> (6 × ₱1.80 = ₱10.80). The total legal fare is <strong>₱23.80</strong>.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 2: Traditional vs Modern Jeepneys -->
			<section class="mb-10 font-sans border-t border-mapa-contour/40 pt-8">
				<h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-4">
					2. Traditional Jeepneys vs. Modern E-Jeepneys: Why the Price Difference?
				</h3>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					Under the DOTr Public Utility Vehicle Modernization Program (PUVMP) and Department Order No. 2017-011, Modern Public Utility Jeepneys (MPUJs) are classified as a higher-tier service category than traditional iconic jeepneys.
				</p>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-4">
					<div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border border-mapa-contour/80">
						<h4 class="font-serif font-bold text-base text-mapa-rust mb-2">Modern PUJs (Class 2 & Class 3)</h4>
						<ul class="text-xs text-mapa-ink/80 space-y-1.5 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-4">
							<li>Base Fare: <strong>₱15.00</strong> (first 4 km) + <strong>₱2.20/km</strong>.</li>
							<li>Air-conditioned cabins, Euro-4 or electric engines.</li>
							<li>Equipped with CCTV, GPS tracking, and automatic fare collection (AFCS) Beep readers.</li>
							<li>Higher capital expenditure and fleet cooperative financing justification.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>

					<div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border border-mapa-contour/80">
						<h4 class="font-serif font-bold text-base text-mapa-green mb-2">Traditional PUJs</h4>
						<ul class="text-xs text-mapa-ink/80 space-y-1.5 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-4">
							<li>Base Fare: <strong>₱13.00</strong> (first 4 km) + <strong>₱1.80/km</strong>.</li>
							<li>Open-air non-airconditioned chassis with parallel bench seating.</li>
							<li>Operated by individual single-unit operators or local transport associations.</li>
							<li>Lower operational baseline reflecting historical tariff benchmarks.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 3: Student & Senior Rights -->
			<section class="mb-10 font-sans border-t border-mapa-contour/40 pt-8">
				<h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-4">
					3. Your 20% Discount Rights Under Philippine Republic Acts
				</h3>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					The 20% passenger fare discount is not an optional courtesy granted by drivers—it is a <strong>mandatory statutory right</strong> enacted by Congress through multiple national laws:
				</p>

				<div class="space-y-4 my-6">
					<div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border-l-4 border-mapa-green border-y border-r border-mapa-contour/80">
						<h4 class="font-serif font-bold text-base text-mapa-green mb-1">
							Republic Act No. 11314 (Student Fare Discount Act)
						</h4>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
							Covers all Filipino students from elementary through college, technical-vocational, and post-secondary institutions. <strong>Crucially, RA 11314 grants the discount 365 days a year—including weekends, summer breaks, semestral vacations, and holidays.</strong> Drivers who refuse the discount on a Sunday or holiday are directly violating national law.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border-l-4 border-mapa-flag-blue border-y border-r border-mapa-contour/80">
						<h4 class="font-serif font-bold text-base text-mapa-flag-blue mb-1">
							Republic Act No. 9994 (Expanded Senior Citizens Act)
						</h4>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
							Grants Filipino resident citizens aged 60 and above a 20% discount on all public land transit (jeepneys, buses, trains, and taxis) upon presentation of an OSCA ID, senior citizen card, or valid government passport/driver's license showing date of birth.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border-l-4 border-mapa-rust border-y border-r border-mapa-contour/80">
						<h4 class="font-serif font-bold text-base text-mapa-rust mb-1">
							Republic Act No. 9442 & RA 10754 (Magna Carta for Persons with Disabilities)
						</h4>
						<p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
							Guarantees a 20% discount and VAT exemption on domestic land, sea, and air transit upon presentation of a valid PWD ID issued by the National Council on Disability Affairs (NCDA) or municipal Persons with Disability Affairs Office (PDAO).
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 4: What to Do If Overcharged -->
			<section class="mb-10 font-sans border-t border-mapa-contour/40 pt-8">
				<h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-4">
					4. What to Do If You Are Overcharged or Denied a Discount
				</h3>
				<p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					Every franchised PUV driver is legally required to post an official laminated copy of the <strong>LTFRB Fare Matrix (*Talaan ng Pamasahe*)</strong> inside the vehicle. If a driver cannot produce or display the matrix, they are barred from collecting rate increases under LTFRB Memorandum Circular No. 2019-035.
				</p>
				<div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border border-mapa-contour space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
					<div class="font-bold text-mapa-green font-serif text-base mb-1">Step-by-Step Overcharging Complaint Procedure:</div>
					<div>1. <strong>Take note of critical identifiers:</strong> The vehicle's license plate number (e.g. ABC 1234), body number, franchise company name, and exact route name.</div>
					<div>2. <strong>Record transaction details:</strong> Note the date, exact time, boarding location, and alighting destination.</div>
					<div>3. <strong>File an official report:</strong>
						<ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-1.5 space-y-1 text-mapa-ink/80">
							<li><strong>LTFRB 24/7 Hotline:</strong> Dial <strong>1342</strong></li>
							<li><strong>LTFRB Viber Complaint Line:</strong> <strong>0928-886-4244</strong></li>
							<li><strong>Official Email:</strong> <code>complaints@ltfrb.gov.ph</code></li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 5: Official Citations -->
			<section class="mb-10 font-sans border-t border-mapa-contour/40 pt-8">
				<h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink mb-4">
					5. Official Citations & Legal Framework
				</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-mapa-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
					<li>
						<strong>LTFRB Board Resolution No. 044, Series of 2023:</strong> Approving calibrated fare adjustments for public utility buses and public utility jeepneys.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Republic Act No. 11314:</strong> An Act Institutionalizing the Grant of Student Fare Discount from Elementary to Post-Secondary Institutions (<a href="https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2019/ra_11314_2019.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-mapa-green hover:underline">Lawphil text</a>).
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Republic Act No. 9994:</strong> The Expanded Senior Citizens Act of 2010.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Republic Act No. 10754:</strong> An Act Expanding the Benefits and Privileges of Persons with Disability (PWD).
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Commonwealth Act No. 146:</strong> The Public Service Act, establishing public carriage tariff regulations.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 6: Editorial Notice -->
			<section class="mb-6 font-sans border-t border-mapa-contour/40 pt-6 text-xs text-mapa-clay">
				<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2">
					<span>Maintained by the <strong>mapaPH Civic Cartography & Transit Initiative</strong>.</span>
